<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Make a Poster! – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Following Instructions at School</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Make a Poster!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of written instructions for classroom activities. Reading: Demonstrate comprehension of basic descriptions in texts. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of written instructions for classroom activities by following them step-by-step (e.g., "I can cut and fold the paper."). Can demonstrate comprehension of basic descriptions in texts by summarizing key details from reading materials (e.g., "The flowchart is colorful."). Can recognize and divide compound and multi-syllabic words into syllables (e.g., "cardboard" into "card-board" and "instructional" into "in-struc-tion-al").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 4px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows realia of school supplies (paper, scissors, glue, crayon, pencil, cardboard) or large flashcards of these items. Students chorally repeat the names of the items. The teacher holds up each item and asks, "What is this?" to review vocabulary from Lesson 1. The teacher then asks, "What can we make with these things?" to connect to the theme "Let's Make a Poster!".</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple instruction card (e.g., "Cut the paper.") and models reading it clearly, pointing to each word. The teacher emphasizes the /p/ sound in "paper" and /s/ in "scissors" as they show the items. The teacher also models dividing a compound word like "classroom" into "class-room" by clapping for each syllable.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we *glue* with *scissors*?" (No!)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a *poster* big or small?" (Big!)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'paper' one sound or two sounds?" (Two sounds for 'pa-per' if practicing syllables, or focus on the whole word pronunciation.)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 4px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 4px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated flowchart with simple written instructions for "How to Make a Simple Paper Card." The flowchart uses sequencing words (first, next) and target vocabulary (paper, fold, cut, glue, decorate, crayon, colorful). Example instructions: "First, take paper." "Next, fold the paper." "Then, cut a shape." "Finally, glue decorations."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension of written instructions and basic descriptions in texts.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher asks, "What are these instructions about?" (Making a card.) Students read the title and look at the pictures to answer.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> The teacher reads simple statements, and students read the flowchart to determine if the statement is true or false. For example: "You need scissors to fold the paper." (False). "First, you glue the paper." (False).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students read short descriptions from the flowchart (e.g., "This paper is red and blue." or "This is a *sharp* *scissor*.") and match them to the corresponding picture in the flowchart. The teacher asks students to identify words like "card-board" or "class-room" if they appear in the visual text or associated descriptions.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 4px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 4px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading written instructions with sequencing words (first, next, then, finally), plural of the imperatives ("Let's cut the paper!"), and target vocabulary (cut, fold, paste, draw, create, paper, scissors, glue, card, colorful, big, small, crayon, paint, cardboard, fabric, string, bead).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ol>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sequencing Sentences:</b> The teacher provides students with strips of paper, each containing one step from the "How to Make a Simple Paper Card" instructions, but in a jumbled order. Students read the strips individually and then work in small groups to arrange them in the correct sequence, using sequencing words as clues. The teacher monitors and helps with decoding and comprehension.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Cloze Activity:</b> Students receive a worksheet with the "How to Make a Simple Paper Card" instructions, but with some action verbs or nouns missing (e.g., "First, ___ (take/glue) the paper." "Next, ___ (fold/cut) the paper."). Students read the sentences and choose the correct word from a provided word bank (e.g., cut, fold, paste, draw, paper, scissors, glue). Teacher reminds students about the /p/ and /s/ sounds in words.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Peer Reading and Check:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading the complete, sequenced instructions from their worksheet aloud to their partner. The listening partner follows along and gives a thumbs up if the instructions are clear and correct. They can practice asking for clarification using "What do I need to do next?" if they don't understand a step their partner reads. Teacher encourages collaborative reading and listening.</li>
        </ol>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 4px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 4px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will read a new set of written instructions for a different classroom art project, such as "How to Make a Paper Mask." They will then work in small groups to read and understand the steps, discussing them collaboratively. The main focus is on reading comprehension and preparing to explain the process, not actually making the mask yet.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students receive a printed sheet with illustrated step-by-step instructions for "How to Make a Paper Mask." The instructions include sequencing words, plural imperatives ("Let's draw!"), and vocabulary like *mask, paper, cut, decorate, string, colorful, big, small*. In groups of 3-4, students first read the instructions silently. Then, they take turns reading one step aloud to the group, summarizing it or clarifying it for their peers. They confirm their understanding of the steps before reporting to the class. Some students might use future tense to talk about the project: "I will make a mask with colorful paper." or "I am going to cut the paper for the mask."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension of multi-step instructions, collaborative reading, understanding sequencing, and using target vocabulary in context. Students practice sociolinguistic competence by collaborating and using "Let's..." for group understanding.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 4px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 4px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Performance stage and a short individual comprehension quiz based on a new reading text.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a short, new set of written instructions or a simple descriptive text about a craft (e.g., "How to Decorate a Cardboard Box" or "My Colorful Project"). They read the text independently. Then, they answer 2-3 multiple-choice questions or fill-in-the-blank questions to show comprehension of the steps, sequencing, and key vocabulary (e.g., "What do you do *first*?" "What color is the *cardboard*?"). A question could also ask to divide a compound word from the text into syllables (e.g., "Divide 'cardboard' into syllables: ____").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in answering questions related to the instructions/description (Reading comprehension).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of sequencing words (first, next).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of key vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives, prepositions).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to divide simple compound words into syllables when prompted.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read instructions and understand what to do." (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I understand words like 'first' and 'next' in instructions." (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 4px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 4px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;">(Student and Teacher reflection)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> (Students answer in their notebooks or orally)</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when reading the instructions today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult for me when reading instructions? What words do I need to practice reading?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well for teaching reading comprehension of instructions? Students seemed to engage with the visual flowcharts and enjoyed the collaborative reading. The sequencing activity was effective. Some students still struggled with decoding longer words or multi-syllabic words like "instructional" or "decoration." I need to incorporate more explicit phonemic awareness practice, especially with syllable division for compound and multi-syllabic words, in future reading activities. Scaffolds for next lesson could include sentence frames for speaking about instructions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Today, we practiced reading instructions. In our next lesson, we will practice *speaking* about these instructions and how to make things, using the vocabulary and sequencing words we learned today. We will move from understanding written instructions to giving and following spoken instructions.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 4px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>